WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.800
What makes the films of
Hayao Miyazaki feel magical?

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.500
Like any great magic trick,

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.525
the secret to Miyazaki's magic

00:00:08.525 --> 00:00:10.658
hides in plain sight.

00:00:10.658 --> 00:00:12.630
In the case of "Spirited Away,"

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.903
in a simple moment such as this.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:20.293
Or this.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.280
On the surface, moments like this

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.471
where nothing happens

00:00:26.471 --> 00:00:28.000
might seem like filler,

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.660
but the six-minute
sequence of "Spirited Away"

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.820
in which nothing really happens

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.810
might best exemplify the secret

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:36.280
to why the world of Miyazaki

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.380
feels so spectacularly magical.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.600
The scene in question occurs
44 minutes into the film,

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.490
the morning after our heroine, Chihiro,

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:48.350
takes a job at a magical
bathhouse for gods and spirits,

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.370
which happens to be
owned by the same witch

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.100
who turned Chihiro's parents into pigs.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.733
Here's how the scene starts.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.510
Haku: Meet me at the bridge.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:03.010
I'll take you to your parents.

00:01:06.639 --> 00:01:07.775
[Chihiro gasps]

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.830
Narrator: Here's a question:

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.730
What shot would you
guess comes after this?

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.393
In most cases, it would look like this:

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.180
cutting straight to the bridge

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.780
where they had promised to meet.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.540
But instead, Miyazaki does this:

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.150
painstakingly showing Chihiro's
journey from the bedroom

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.950
all the way to the bridge,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:33.130
all locations that we've seen
her pass the other way around.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.130
This is why Miyazaki's
world feels so real.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.070
Because as magical as it is,

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:40.243
there is a sense of space portrayed

00:01:40.243 --> 00:01:43.200
through time, distance, and scale.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.010
That's why a quick moment
that introduces a location

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:49.890
like this is so simple, yet genius.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.430
Because despite being a location
we've never seen before,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.340
by the time Chihiro gets there,

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.040
we know exactly where it is

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.430
compared to the rest of the world.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.920
Going back to the story,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.220
this specific six-minute sequence

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.230
is what's referred to as
a pinch in a screenplay -

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:07.820
a moment that occurs approximately

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.860
three-eighths of the way into a script

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.940
that gives the audience
a moment to breathe

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.489
and reminds them what's going on,

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:16.730
like all the side
characters that will later

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.810
play a vital role in the story

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.230
and, most importantly, what's at stake.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.500
The short interaction between
Haku and Chihiro at the farm

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.110
is full of these reminders
of what's at stake.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.301
Like the fate of Chihiro's parents.

00:02:30.301 --> 00:02:31.253
Chihiro: Mom!

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:32.169
Dad!

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.400
What's wrong with them?

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.270
Haku: They don't remember being human.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.720
So look hard. It's up to you

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.320
to remember which ones they are.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.839
Narrator: Or Chihiro's own identity.

00:02:42.839 --> 00:02:45.830
Chihiro: Chihiro. That's
my name, isn't it?

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:48.517
Haku: That's how Yubaba controls you,

00:02:48.517 --> 00:02:50.440
by stealing your name.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.280
Narrator: These reminders
are Miyazaki's way

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.200
of easily guiding the
audience through the journey,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.260
letting them know
exactly what has happened

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.140
and also where it's heading.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.480
Which leads us to the moment

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.997
that truly makes the scene magical.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.950
Miyazaki's films are famous
for their culinary scenes,

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:19.060
showing characters either
cooking or eating in silence.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.440
It's never just a dining scene,

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.090
but rather a moment when
characters bond with one another

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.500
and also a moment that
Miyazaki refers to as "ma,"

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.030
meaning emptiness in Japanese.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:32.380
In one of his interviews,

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.502
Miyazaki describes it as
the time between a clap,

00:03:35.502 --> 00:03:36.477
[claps]

00:03:36.477 --> 00:03:37.632
explaining:

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.320
You can actually find
these instances of ma

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.570
in all of Miyazaki's films.

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.340
A moment when time just seems to stop.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.720
Nothing really happens,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.810
yet you feel yourself
absorbed by its world.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.780
Sometimes it's just watching someone

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.470
eat a rice ball in tears.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:08.240
In a sense, this entire
six-minute sequence

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.250
is a perfect example of Miyazaki's ma.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.030
Nothing really happens.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:16.270
It's just six minutes of
a world we'd already seen,

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:18.860
reminding us of the story we already know,

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:22.470
watching things that happen
that have no real consequences.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.360
Yet it's these moments that make

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.400
the films of Miyazaki so magical.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.120
As exciting as its final reveal is,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.610
what makes a magic trick so special

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.693
is the anticipation that precedes it.

